TITLE:: ParaEQ
summary:: a five band parametric eq with a high pass and a low pass, as a UGen and as an object that drives it
categories:: Libraries>ParaEQ
related:: Classes/FxUnit, Classes/FxChain, Classes/ParaEQGUI, Classes/ParaEQPanel, Classes/ParaEQView, Classes/ParaEQSpectrum, Classes/ModuleEQ, Classes/BLowShelf, Classes/BPeakEQ, Classes/BHiShelf, Classes/BLowPass, Classes/BHiPass

DESCRIPTION::
Five biquads in series - low shelf, three peaks, high shelf - each band a
code::[freq, dB, q]:: triple, and either end of the spectrum cut away as well
when you ask for it: a high pass and a low pass, both out until they are
switched in. The filter is one pseudo-UGen, link::#*ar::, so it runs wherever a
UGen does; and an instance of this class drives it wherever it runs: a control
on an Ndef or a Synth, a node of its own on a bus, or a place in an
link::Classes/FxChain::.

code::
s.boot;
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar([110, 110.7]) * 0.1; ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq) }).play;

e = ParaEQ(Ndef(\pad), \eq);      // drives that Ndef's \eq control
e.gui;
e.preset = \telephone;
e.setBand(0, 80, -9, 0.8);        // low shelf, 80 Hz, -9 dB, rs 0.8
::

The same object with a node of its own, in place on a bus:

code::
e = ParaEQ.onBus(0, 2);
e.enabled = true;
::

Everything about how it is driven - targets, buses, chains, what
code::enabled:: does in each case - is link::Classes/FxUnit::'s and documented
there. This file is about the eq.

subsection:: Bands and cuts

A band is code::[freq, dB, q]::, numbered 0 to 4. code::q:: is an code::rs:: on
the two shelves and an code::rq:: on the three peaks - smaller is narrower - and
the floor differs accordingly: 0.6 for a shelf, 0.05 for a peak. Gain is clipped
to ± link::#*dbRange::, frequency to link::#*minFreq:: .. link::#*maxFreq::.
Inside those limits the filters stay well behaved.

After the bands come the two cuts, and the window numbers them all together:
seven strong::handles::, 0 to 6, which is the number every method here that
takes an code::i:: wants.

table::
## strong::i:: || strong::what:: || strong::held as::
## 0 .. 4 || low shelf, peak 1, peak 2, peak 3, high shelf || code::[freq, dB, q]::
## 5 || high pass - link::#*highPass:: || code::[freq, rq, in]::
## 6 || low pass - link::#*lowPass:: || code::[freq, rq, in]::
::

A cut has no gain: it is a switch, and both cuts are out until you switch one
in, so an eq that has never been told about them is the eq it always was.
code::rq:: defaults to 1.414 - the flattest a cut goes, 3 dB down at its own
frequency and no bump before it - and goes down to link::#*cutQLo::, where the
corner is a resonant peak. Out, a cut is out however its frequency has been
left.

Each cut has a slope, how hard it cuts: 6 to 96 dB per octave in steps of 6, 12
unless you say otherwise, and a value between steps goes to the nearest one. A
filter only comes in whole orders, 6 dB per octave each, and every one of them here
is a Butterworth filter 3 dB down at the corner, so a step only changes how hard it
falls away. 12 is one biquad, the filter the cuts always were. Nothing fades when
the slope changes, so moving it on a playing sound can click. At 6 there is no
biquad for code::rq:: to work on; above it, code::rq:: works on the narrowest stage.
Very steep slopes ring at the corner.

code::
e = ParaEQ.onBus(0, 2);
e.enabled = true;
e.highPassOn = true;              // the rumble gone
e.highPassFreq = 60;
e.highPassSlope = 48;             // and hard
e.lowPassOn = true; e.lowPassFreq = 12000;
e.cutOn_(ParaEQ.highPass, false); // and back out
e.free;
::

Changes reach the node immediately and are lagged there by link::#*lag::
seconds, so dragging a handle is a slide rather than a step - and switching a
cut in is a fade over the same time rather than a click.

subsection:: The curve is the filter

link::#-responseAt:: works the dB curve out from the same coefficients the
server runs - link::#*coeffs:: is the BEQSuite arithmetic in numbers, and
link::#*cutCoeffs:: the stages of a cut at its slope - so the window draws the filter you hear, not a sketch of it. That is the one thing this
class has to get right: change link::#*ar:: and link::#*coeffs:: together.

subsection:: Presets and the clipboard

Every eq shares one preset list - see link::#*presetNames:: - and one clipboard,
so a curve saved on an Ndef's eq is in the menu of a OneShot pad's, and a curve
copied on a pad pastes onto the master chain's. Your own presets are kept in
link::#*presetFile::, in the support folder rather than the repository.

subsection:: The levels a window shows

A window - link::Classes/ParaEQGUI::, any link::Classes/ParaEQPanel:: - shows the
peak going into the eq and coming out of it, beside the curve. While it is open it
switches link::#-meter:: on, and the node says its levels link::#*meterRate:: times
a second, tagged with link::#-id:: so each eq hears its own. The eq's own node and a
chain's always can; a graph of your own only when it asks:

code::
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar([110, 110.7]) * 0.1; ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq, meter: true) });
::

Left out, the graph is exactly the one it always was - and the replies change
nothing in the sound either way: they sit beside the filters, silent until a window
asks (proved by a null test of the old def against the new one: not one sample
differs). SuperDirtGUI's eq insert is built with them. A holder of settings with no
node - the compressor's sidechain eq - never has levels, and its window leaves the
meters out.

subsection:: The window's words

The window says what a handle does rather than what filter it is: the high pass is
the strong::low cut::, it takes the lows away; the low pass the strong::high cut::.
A bell's width reads in octaves between the points where it has half its gain -
link::#*bellOctaves::, 1.39 octaves at code::rq:: 1 - and a shelf's as its
code::rs:: in percent. A cut's code::res:: is how its corner rings, -1 to 1:
link::#*cutRes::, 0 the flat corner. The code is unchanged: link::#-qAt:: is still
the code::rq:: or code::rs:: the server runs.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar
The filter, as a UGen.

ARGUMENT:: in
The signal, any number of channels.

ARGUMENT:: name
The control the bands arrive on: fifteen numbers, code::[freq, q, dB]:: per band,
made with link::Classes/NamedControl::. Two more go with it: code::name:: with
code::Cut:: appended carries the two cuts, code::[freq, rq, in]:: each,
code::name:: with code::Slope:: appended their two slopes, and
code::name:: with code::On:: appended crossfades the whole thing to dry. A node
built before the cuts existed still takes the bands, since they travel on a
control of their own.

A stereo eq is about 200 UGens and 11 KB of def, because a graph holds everything
the steepest slope needs, whatever the slope is. Measured on a stock server: 51 KB
of real time memory and 0.36% of a core per stereo eq.

ARGUMENT:: bypass
code::false:: leaves the code::On:: control out, for a node that is freed rather
than switched - what link::#*synthDef:: and ModuleCommon's slot def do.

ARGUMENT:: lag
Seconds the bands are lagged by; link::#*lag:: if left out.

ARGUMENT:: meter
code::true:: adds the levels a window shows: the loudest channel going in and
coming out, on code::'/paraeq'::, while code::name:: with code::Meter:: appended is
1, tagged with code::name:: with code::Id:: appended - see "The levels a window
shows" above. Nothing in the sound changes. Left out
(code::false::), the graph is byte for byte the one it was before there were
meters, which is what every graph written before them, the compressor's and
ModuleCommon's still are.

RETURNS:: The filtered signal, through link::Classes/Sanitize::.

METHOD:: new
Drive a control on a node. See link::Classes/FxUnit#*new::.

ARGUMENT:: target
An Ndef, a Synth, a Group - anything that answers to code::set:: - or nil for an
eq something else pushes.

ARGUMENT:: controlName
code::\eq:: by default.

METHOD:: onBus
A node of its own, in place on a bus. See link::Classes/FxUnit#*onBus::.

METHOD:: defName
METHOD:: synthDef
The in place synth for a channel count: code::In:: - link::#*ar:: - code::XOut::
under an envelope, so switching in and out leaves the dry signal rather than
silence. It is built with the meter, silent until a window asks.

METHOD:: meterRate
How many times a second a node with the meter says its levels while a window shows
them. 25.

METHOD:: replyName
code::'/paraeq'::, what the levels arrive on.

METHOD:: meterName
METHOD:: idName
The meter's two controls for a control name: code::\eqMeter:: and code::\eqId:: for
code::\eq::.
ARGUMENT:: name

METHOD:: title
METHOD:: windowSize
METHOD:: identityColour
What its window is called (code::"eq"::), how big it opens (960 x 560) and its
colour: ivory, the summed curve - the bands have the hues.

METHOD:: shapeWord
What the window calls a handle: code::"low shelf"::, code::"bell"::,
code::"high shelf"::, code::"low cut"::, code::"high cut"::.
ARGUMENT:: i

METHOD:: cutWord
code::"low cut":: for link::#*highPass::, code::"high cut":: for link::#*lowPass::.
ARGUMENT:: i

METHOD:: handleColour
A handle's colour, low to high: violet, teal, lime, yellow, pink, and the cuts'
silver - none of them a colour that means something else in the window.
ARGUMENT:: i

METHOD:: bellOctaves
METHOD:: bellRq
A bell's width in octaves between its half-gain points, from its code::rq:: at a
frequency and a rate - RBJ's bandwidth with the bilinear warp, so it is the filter
the server runs: 1.39 at code::rq:: 1, a little less near the top - and back.
ARGUMENT:: rq
ARGUMENT:: octaves
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: sr

METHOD:: cutRq
METHOD:: cutRes
A cut's corner as the window shows it, code::res:: -1 to 1, and the code::rq:: it
runs on. 0 is 1.414, the flat corner; up to 1 rings, down to link::#*cutQLo::; down to -1 rounds
off, up to 10. Logarithmic both ways.
ARGUMENT:: res
ARGUMENT:: rq

METHOD:: numBands
5 - the parametric bands.

METHOD:: numCuts
2 - the high pass and the low pass.

METHOD:: numHandles
7 - the bands and the cuts together, which is what a window shows and what an
code::i:: counts through.

METHOD:: isCut
Whether a handle index is one of the two cuts.

METHOD:: highPass
METHOD:: lowPass
Their handle indices: 5 and 6.

METHOD:: bandNames
code::["low shelf", "peak 1", "peak 2", "peak 3", "high shelf"]::.

METHOD:: cutNames
code::["high pass", "low pass"]::.

METHOD:: bandName
The name of one handle, cuts included.

METHOD:: bandTypes
code::\lowShelf::, code::\peak:: ×3, code::\hiShelf::.

METHOD:: cutTypes
code::\hiPass::, code::\loPass::.

METHOD:: isShelf
Whether a handle index is one of the two shelves.

METHOD:: qLo
METHOD:: qHi
The width limits for a handle: 0.6 for a shelf, 0.05 for a peak,
link::#*cutQLo:: for a cut, 10 at the top for all of them.

METHOD:: cutQLo
How narrow a cut may be made. 0.1 by default; below that the corner is a whistle
rather than a filter.

METHOD:: defaultBands
METHOD:: defaultCuts
METHOD:: flatSettings
A fresh copy of the flat setting: the five bands, the two cuts, or both
together.

METHOD:: dbRange
How far a band may be pushed either way, and the range the curve is drawn over.
24 by default.

METHOD:: minFreq
METHOD:: maxFreq
20 and 20000 by default.

METHOD:: lag
How quickly the filters follow a change, in seconds. 0.1 by default.

METHOD:: fade
How long a node of its own takes to fade in and out when it is switched. 0.02.

METHOD:: presetNames
Every preset, the ones the library ships first: code::\flat::, code::'rumble
cut'::, code::\warm::, code::\bright::, code::\distant::, code::\loudness::,
code::\telephone::, then three that use the cuts - code::\radio::, code::'kick
weight'::, code::\air:: - then yours.

METHOD:: presets
METHOD:: userPresets
METHOD:: allPresets
The same as code::name, bands, name, bands...::.

METHOD:: presetBands
A copy of one preset's bands, or nil.

METHOD:: isUserPreset
Whether a name is yours, and so can be written over or dropped.

METHOD:: addPreset
Keep a curve under a name. It joins the menu of every window and is written to
link::#*presetFile::. A name the library ships is refused, and so is a curve that
is neither seven handles long - the bands with the two cuts after them, which is
what link::#-settings:: hands you - nor the five bands alone, which is what a
preset saved before the cuts existed holds. Answers the name, or nil.

code::
ParaEQ.addPreset(\scooped,
    [[80, 3, 0.8], [250, 0, 1], [900, -8, 1.2], [3500, 0, 1], [8000, 4, 0.8]]);
ParaEQ.removePreset(\scooped);
::

METHOD:: removePreset
Drop one of yours.

METHOD:: clearUserPresets
Drop all of yours, on disk as well.

METHOD:: presetFile
Where your presets are kept - code::paraeq-presets.scd:: in
link::Classes/Platform#*userAppSupportDir::. Settable. ModuleEQ's older
code::module-eq-presets.scd:: is read once if this one does not exist yet.

METHOD:: presetStore
The link::Classes/FxPresetStore:: behind all of the above.

METHOD:: clipboard
The curve link::#-copyBands:: last put aside, or nil.

METHOD:: hasClipboard
Whether there is anything to paste.

METHOD:: coeffs
One biquad as code::[a0, a1, a2, b1, b2]:: - the shape BEQSuite hands the
server, in numbers.

ARGUMENT:: type
code::\lowShelf::, code::\peak::, code::\hiShelf::, code::\hiPass:: or
code::\loPass::.
ARGUMENT:: sr
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: q
ARGUMENT:: db

METHOD:: magnitudes
code::|H(e^jw)|:: of one set of coefficients at every frequency in an Array.

METHOD:: cutCoeffs
The stages of one cut at a slope, as a list of what link::#*coeffs:: answers, the
first-order ones with no third term. The product of their link::#*magnitudes:: is
the cut.

ARGUMENT:: type
code::\hiPass:: or code::\loPass::.
ARGUMENT:: sr
The sample rate.
ARGUMENT:: freq
The corner, in Hz.
ARGUMENT:: rq
The cut's rq.
ARGUMENT:: slope
In dB per octave, taken to the nearest step.

METHOD:: defaultSlope
METHOD:: slopeStep
METHOD:: slopeLo
METHOD:: slopeHi
METHOD:: slopes
A cut's slope in dB per octave: 12 unless told otherwise, from 6 to 96 in steps of
6. code::slopes:: is all of them, as an Array.

METHOD:: nearestSlope
The step a value is nearest to.

ARGUMENT:: v
A number of dB per octave.

METHOD:: flatControls
METHOD:: flatCutControls
METHOD:: flatSlopeControls
The node's defaults: fifteen numbers, flat, the six the cuts arrive on with both
of them out, and the two slopes.

METHOD:: cutName
The name of the control the cuts arrive on, from the name the bands do:
code::ParaEQ.cutName(\eq):: is code::\eqCut::.

METHOD:: slopeName
The same for the slopes: code::ParaEQ.slopeName(\eq):: is code::\eqSlope::.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: bands
The five code::[freq, dB, q]:: triples. Setting takes a list of them and pushes
all five at once; a list of seven carries the two cuts after them, which is what
link::#-settings:: and a preset hold, and a list of five leaves the cuts where
they are.

METHOD:: cuts
The two code::[freq, rq, in]:: triples, high pass first, with the slope as a
fourth number on a cut whose slope is not the default. Setting takes a list of
them, and a cut with no slope in it goes back to the default.

METHOD:: setBand
Move one handle. nil leaves that part of it where it was. code::db:: is ignored
on a cut, which has none - link::#-cutOn:: is what a cut has in its place.

ARGUMENT:: i
0..6 - see link::#Bands and cuts::.
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: db
ARGUMENT:: q

METHOD:: freqAt
METHOD:: dbAt
METHOD:: qAt
One part of one handle, with a two argument setter each: code::dbAt_(4, 3)::. A
cut has no gain, so its dB reads 0 and setting it does nothing.

METHOD:: widthAt
A band's width as the window shows it, with a two argument setter: a bell's in
octaves (link::#*bellOctaves::, at the server's rate), a shelf's code::rs::. nil on a
cut.

METHOD:: resAt
How much a cut's corner rings, -1 to 1 (link::#*cutRes::), with a two argument
setter. nil on a band.

METHOD:: slopeAt
How hard the cut at that handle index cuts, in dB per octave, with a two argument
setter: code::slopeAt_(5, 48)::. nil on a band, and setting it on one does nothing.
code::highPassSlope:: and code::lowPassSlope:: are the same two under their own
names.

METHOD:: cutOn
Whether the cut at that handle index is in. A band always is, so this answers
true for one and setting it on one does nothing.

code::
e = ParaEQ.new;
e.cutOn(ParaEQ.highPass);         // -> false
e.cutOn_(ParaEQ.highPass, true);
e.toggleCut(ParaEQ.highPass);
::

METHOD:: toggleCut
That cut in if it was out, out if it was in.

METHOD:: highPassOn
METHOD:: highPassFreq
METHOD:: highPassQ
METHOD:: lowPassOn
METHOD:: lowPassFreq
METHOD:: lowPassQ
The same two cuts under their own names, a setter each.

METHOD:: flat
Every band back to flat and both cuts out - the whole eq doing nothing.

METHOD:: preset
Set the bands from a preset, by name.

METHOD:: presetName
The preset these bands are, or nil once you have moved one.

METHOD:: saveAsPreset
Keep this curve under a name - link::#*addPreset::.

METHOD:: copyBands
METHOD:: pasteBands
The shared clipboard. Only the curve moves, not whether the eq is on.

METHOD:: copyFrom
The same straight from another eq.

METHOD:: responseAt
The dB curve of every handle over an Array of frequencies - the five bands, then
the two cuts, each of those flat while it is out. Sum them for the curve you
hear.

METHOD:: controls
METHOD:: cutControls
What the node is given: fifteen numbers, code::[freq, q, dB]:: per band, and six
more, code::[freq, rq, in]:: per cut, on the control link::#-cutName:: answers.

METHOD:: cutName
The control the cuts arrive on: code::\eqCut:: for an eq driving code::\eq::.

METHOD:: settings
The five bands and the two cuts after them, for a preset or the clipboard -
link::Classes/FxUnit#-settings::.

METHOD:: asDict
METHOD:: fromDict
code::(enabled:, bands:, cuts:):: - the shape ModuleCommon's kits and sets have
always written, with the cuts alongside it. One written before the cuts existed
has no code::cuts:: and reads back as an eq with both of them out.

METHOD:: sampleRate
The server's, when it is up - what the curve is worked out at.

METHOD:: meter
Whether the node says its levels. A window switches it on while it is open and off
when it closes; changed(\levels) on every reply.

METHOD:: canMeter
Whether there is anything that could say them: a node of its own, a chain, a driven
target. A holder of settings never can.

METHOD:: inLevel
METHOD:: outLevel
The peak going in and coming out, 0 .. 1 and over, while replies arrive; 0 once a
node has stopped saying them (switched off, freed, paused).

METHOD:: isAlive
Whether a reply came in the last half second.

METHOD:: hasReplied
Whether anything came back since the meter was switched on: a graph built without
code::meter: true:: never answers.

METHOD:: lastReply
When the last reply came, in code::Main.elapsedTime::.

METHOD:: id
The number this eq's node tags its replies with.

METHOD:: meterName
METHOD:: idName
The meter's controls under this eq's control name.

METHOD:: enabled
METHOD:: gui
METHOD:: panel
METHOD:: target
METHOD:: chain
METHOD:: synth
See link::Classes/FxUnit::.

EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;

// in a graph
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar([110, 110.7]) * 0.1; ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq) }).play;
e = ParaEQ(Ndef(\pad), \eq);
e.gui;
e.preset = \loudness;
e.setBand(0, 60, -18, 0.8);       // lose the rumble
e.dbAt_(4, 4);                    // put some air back
e.enabled = false;                // dry, crossfaded
e.enabled = true;

// either end taken off
e.highPassOn = true;
e.highPassFreq = 80;              // nothing under 80 Hz
e.lowPassOn = true;
e.lowPassFreq = 9000;
e.lowPassQ = 0.4;                 // narrow enough to ring at the corner
e.flat;                           // bands flat, both cuts out again

// kept, and carried
e.saveAsPreset("my room");
e.copyBands;

// on the main out, in place
m = ParaEQ.onBus(0, 2);
m.enabled = true;
m.pasteBands;
m.gui;

m.free;
e.free;
Ndef(\pad).clear;
ParaEQ.removePreset("my room");
::
